      "title": "What HIPAA set out to fix",
      "narration": "Before 1996, a worker who changed jobs could face a new waiting period for conditions she already had, and a small employer could be turned down for coverage because one employee was sick. The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act addressed both. It limited pre-existing condition exclusions in group plans and gave credit for prior coverage, required insurers to issue coverage to small employers and renew group coverage, and created special enrollment rights. It also produced the privacy and security rules that govern how health information is handled. The Affordable Care Act later went further, but HIPAA’s framework remains on the exam.",
